{"subjectId":"java","id":"recursion","title":"Recursion","group":"Core Language: Strings, Arrays & Methods","order":17,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A method that calls itself on a smaller input. Every recursion needs a <strong>base case</strong>; each call pushes a stack frame, so depth is limited.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Recursion</strong> — solving a problem by reducing it to smaller instances of the same problem, until a <em>base case</em> can be answered directly.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stairs\"></i>The two parts</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static long factorial(int n) {\n        if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;                 // base case\n        return n * factorial(n - 1);          // recursive case: smaller input\n    }\n\n    static int sumDigits(int n) {\n        return n &lt; 10 ? n : n % 10 + sumDigits(n / 10);\n    }\n\n    static String reverse(String s) {\n        return s.isEmpty() ? \"\" : reverse(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(factorial(5) + \" \" + factorial(20));\n        System.out.println(sumDigits(9875));\n        System.out.println(reverse(\"stack\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>120 2432902008176640000\n29\nkcats</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>The call stack</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>factorial(3)</code> called</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Frame pushed: <code>n=3</code>, waiting on <code>factorial(2)</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>factorial(2)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Frame pushed: <code>n=2</code>, waiting on <code>factorial(1)</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>factorial(1)</code> — base case</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Returns <code>1</code>; frame popped.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Unwinding</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>2 * 1 = 2</code> returned, then <code>3 * 2 = 6</code>. Each frame pops as it returns.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int depth = 0;\n\n    static void dive() {\n        depth++;\n        dive();                               // no base case\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try {\n            dive();\n        } catch (StackOverflowError e) {      // an Error, not an Exception\n            System.out.println(\"StackOverflowError after \" + (depth &gt; 1000 ? \"thousands of\" : \"few\") + \" calls\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>StackOverflowError after thousands of calls</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>The JVM does <strong>not</strong> perform tail-call optimization. Even a tail-recursive method uses one frame per call, so deep recursion (e.g. 100 000 levels) overflows the default ~512 KB–1 MB thread stack. Convert to a loop, or run in a thread with a bigger stack.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Memoization: from exponential to linear</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int calls = 0;\n\n    static long fibNaive(int n) {\n        calls++;\n        return n &lt; 2 ? n : fibNaive(n - 1) + fibNaive(n - 2);    // O(2^n)\n    }\n\n    static Map&lt;Integer, Long&gt; memo = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();\n    static long fibMemo(int n) {\n        if (n &lt; 2) return n;\n        Long cached = memo.get(n);\n        if (cached != null) return cached;\n        long v = fibMemo(n - 1) + fibMemo(n - 2);                // O(n)\n        memo.put(n, v);\n        return v;\n    }\n\n    static long fibIter(int n) {                                 // O(n) time, O(1) space\n        long a = 0, b = 1;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { long t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }\n        return a;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(fibNaive(25) + \" in \" + calls + \" calls\");\n        System.out.println(fibMemo(90) + \" \" + fibIter(90));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>75025 in 242785 calls\n2880067194370816120 2880067194370816120</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Gotcha</div><p>Don't use <code>memo.computeIfAbsent(n, k -&gt; fib(k-1) + fib(k-2))</code> for recursive memoization on a <code>HashMap</code> — modifying the map inside its own <code>computeIfAbsent</code> throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> (Java 9+).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i>Classic recursive patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int binarySearch(int[] a, int target, int lo, int hi) {\n        if (lo &gt; hi) return -1;\n        int mid = (lo + hi) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1;\n        if (a[mid] == target) return mid;\n        return a[mid] &lt; target ? binarySearch(a, target, mid + 1, hi)\n                               : binarySearch(a, target, lo, mid - 1);\n    }\n\n    static void subsets(int[] nums, int i, Deque&lt;Integer&gt; cur, List&lt;String&gt; out) {\n        if (i == nums.length) { out.add(cur.toString()); return; }\n        subsets(nums, i + 1, cur, out);            // exclude nums[i]\n        cur.addLast(nums[i]);\n        subsets(nums, i + 1, cur, out);            // include nums[i]\n        cur.removeLast();                          // backtrack\n    }\n\n    static void hanoi(int n, char from, char to, char via, List&lt;String&gt; moves) {\n        if (n == 0) return;\n        hanoi(n - 1, from, via, to, moves);\n        moves.add(from + \"&gt;\" + to);\n        hanoi(n - 1, via, to, from, moves);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(binarySearch(new int[]{1, 3, 5, 7, 9}, 7, 0, 4));\n        List&lt;String&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        subsets(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 0, new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;(), out);\n        System.out.println(out);\n        List&lt;String&gt; moves = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        hanoi(3, 'A', 'C', 'B', moves);\n        System.out.println(moves.size() + \" moves: \" + moves);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3\n[[], [3], [2], [2, 3], [1], [1, 3], [1, 2], [1, 2, 3]]\n7 moves: [A&gt;C, A&gt;B, C&gt;B, A&gt;C, B&gt;A, B&gt;C, A&gt;C]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Recursion</th><th>Iteration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Natural for trees, graphs, divide &amp; conquer, backtracking</td><td>Natural for linear scans</td></tr><tr><td>O(depth) stack space</td><td>O(1) extra space usually</td></tr><tr><td>Risk of <code>StackOverflowError</code></td><td>No stack risk</td></tr><tr><td>Method-call overhead per step</td><td>Faster in the JVM</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if a recursive method has no base case?</summary><div><p>It recurses until the thread's stack is exhausted and the JVM throws <code>StackOverflowError</code> (a subclass of <code>VirtualMachineError</code>, i.e. an <code>Error</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does Java optimize tail recursion?</summary><div><p>No. The JVM spec has no tail-call elimination (partly to keep stack traces and security checks intact), so rewrite deep tail recursion as a loop.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you find the time complexity of a recursive function?</summary><div><p>Write the recurrence: e.g. naive Fibonacci <code>T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2) + O(1)</code> → O(2ⁿ) (tighter: φⁿ); binary search <code>T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1)</code> → O(log n); merge sort <code>T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)</code> → O(n log n) (Master theorem).</p></div></details></section>"}